    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "WHAT THIS FACT CLAIMS AND WHAT F:int-categoricity ADDS. These properties are proved to HOLD of `Int`. Until 2026-08-18 it was NOT proved that they determine `Int` up to isomorphism -- the existence half of the universal property, a map `Int -> R` built from an arbitrary target's own data, was missing, so `rec_unique` was uniqueness of a map nobody had constructed. That half is now built (`Int.Characterization.iter` with `iter_zero`/`iter_succ`/`iter_pred`) and the categoricity theorem that follows from it is F:int-categoricity, which supersedes this fact's former caveat. The categoricity there is over Z-STRUCTURES (a point with an automorphism), not over discretely ordered rings: the order properties recorded HERE are what separate Z from Q and from lexicographic Z[x], and they are proved of `Int` rather than assumed of an arbitrary target. Ordered-ring laws alone pin nothing (the rationals satisfy every one of them), which is why the no-junk, generation and discreteness rows are the substance here and the ordered-ring rows are not restated as this fact's evidence.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
      "provenance": {
        "command": "python3 render/producers-py/facts_to_docir.py",
        "epoch": {
